{
 "slug": "expanding-and-factorising",
 "title": "Expanding and Factorising: Single and Double Brackets",
 "seo_title": "Year 8 Expanding and Factorising Worksheets: Single Brackets, Double Brackets and Common Factors",
 "short_title": "Expanding and factorising",
 "domain": "Algebra",
 "standard_code": "KS3: Algebra",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/national-curriculum-in-england-mathematics-programmes-of-study",
 "standard_text": "Simplify and manipulate algebraic expressions to maintain equivalence by: collecting like terms; multiplying a single term over a bracket; taking out common factors; expanding products of two or more binomials.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 8 worksheets on expanding and factorising: single brackets with letters outside, expanding and simplifying, double brackets, factorising with algebraic common factors and area problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Expand: multiply every term inside the bracket. Factorise: take out the highest common factor.",
 "key_sub": "For two brackets, multiply each term in the first by each term in the second, then collect like terms.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>To <b>expand</b>, multiply every term inside the bracket by the term outside. For two brackets, multiply every term in the first by every term in the second (a grid helps), then collect like terms.</p><p>To <b>factorise</b>, find the highest common factor of all the terms, including letters, and take it outside the bracket. Check by expanding.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Expand 3<i>x</i>(2<i>x</i> − 5).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<i>x</i> × 2<i>x</i> = 6<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<i>x</i> × (−5) = −15<i>x</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 6<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 15<i>x</i></p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Expand and simplify (<i>x</i> + 4)(<i>x</i> − 2).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 2<i>x</i> + 4<i>x</i> − 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>x</i> − 8</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Factorise fully 6<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 9<i>x</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>The HCF of 6<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> and 9<i>x</i> is 3<i>x</i>.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3<i>x</i>(2<i>x</i> + 3)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying only the first term: 3<i>x</i>(2<i>x</i> − 5) is not 6<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 5.</li><li>Losing the sign: −2(<i>x</i> − 3) = −2<i>x</i> + 6.</li><li>Missing the middle terms: (<i>x</i> + 4)<sup>2</sup> = <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 8<i>x</i> + 16, not <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 16.</li><li>Not factorising fully: 12<i>x</i> + 18 = 6(2<i>x</i> + 3), not 2(6<i>x</i> + 9).</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the difference between expanding and factorising?",
   "a": "Expanding removes brackets by multiplying out. Factorising puts brackets back in by taking out a common factor. They are opposites."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I expand double brackets?",
   "a": "Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second (four products), then collect like terms. A grid or FOIL helps."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does factorise fully mean?",
   "a": "Take out the highest common factor, including any letters, so nothing more can come out of the bracket."
  },
  {
   "q": "How can I check my factorising?",
   "a": "Expand your answer. You should get back to the expression you started with."
  }
 ],
